    "created_at": "2023-11-23T09:27:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-11-23T09:27:50Z",
    "body": "Making the case for using traversal rather than reload as the navigation type for this scenario:\r\n1. Conceptually, what's happening here is a restore from history. Perhaps \"back_forward\" or \"traversal\" is a poor name for it, but the essence is the same: we load the history, and then traverse to a particular session in it.\r\n1. If BFCache supports persistence to disk in some implementation as some point, this could be utilized here: a session restore doesn't only restore the history, but even restores it entire JS state etc.\r\n1. I could be missing a scenario, but as a general rule a `reload` has an active document when it's invoked. This is observable - if you put the timing of your `pagehide` into `sessionStorage`, you should observe if it was there upon reload. So if you don't see a pagehide that coincide with the start time of this reload, this is very likely a tab restore. The same is not true for marking it as traversal, you could be going to a page that had been in history and was evicted (this is essentially what happened anyway...)",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
